var FULL_ANSWERS = "<details class=\"full\">\n        <summary><span class=\"q\">At what times in your life were you the happiest, and why?</span> <span class=\"more\">Read \u2192</span></summary>\n        <div class=\"body\">\n          <p>When I look back on my life I believe that I was happiest during elementary school when I had a lot of same-age friends to play with on Isabell Circle in Randolph, Ma. We rode bikes, had a mile or so of woods just behind my house where we played \"the game in the woods,\" our invention, a mixture of hide and seek and tag. We broke into two teams of 4–6 kids. One team had a few minutes to disperse anywhere within a quarter mile from \"Gools,\" a huge rock that was left from the Ice Age, I suspect. One kid would guard the rock while the others on defense would go in different directions to find the team hiding. The defenders had to tag the kids hiding in the woods before the kids could run back to the big rock and jump onto it, thus winning a point. So, it wasn't enough to see the kid who was hiding. You also had to catch him and tag him before he climbed onto the rock. It was a very fun and creative game.</p>\n          <p>If you were on the team hiding in the woods, you could climb a tree in the pine forest area. You could lure the defenders into the thorn bushes where they'd get stuck while you ran to the rock. You could devise a plan where two or three of you hid together, and if a defender came by, you'd all run toward the rock at the same time, hoping that the defender couldn't tag more than one of you, or have time to call out for other defenders to help corral all of you.</p>\n          <p>One summer, we built our own baseball field behind the neighborhood. The field was very rocky and uneven, after we'd raked and tried our best to make the infield flat. First base had a huge rock behind it which we'd run up on after getting a single. In short right field were a bunch of immovable boulders, one of which I tripped over while backpedaling to catch a pop up. I fell and broke my left arm, which I remember to this day. I had to go to the family doctor. The waiting room was full, and the adults there saw that I was in a lot of pain, and let me cut in line to see the doctor first. Of course, he sent me to the local hospital, a Catholic one where my arm was set. The Carney Hospital. It had crosses above the beds. I didn't mind at all. I got to wear a cast for over a month. Everyone signed it. I used the time to learn how to shoot a basketball and throw a baseball with my opposite hand, my right hand.</p>\n          <p>We also had a paved basketball court two streets over at the Carlinos' house. The Carlinos let us play ball whenever we wanted to. I'd take my pet cat Snowball on a walk over there while I practiced. The cat hung around and walked home with me.</p>\n          <p>We also played \"Hit the stick,\" which involved batting a rubber ball on the street. Whoever caught the ball rolled it towards the bat that was placed on the ground in the middle of the street. If the ball hit the bat and bounced off the bat to the ground before the batter could catch it, the person who rolled the ball into the bat became the next hitter. If the roller missed the bat or the batter caught the ball that hit the bat on the roll, he remained the batter.</p>\n          <p>We had a great Hide and Seek area up the street. We'd play at night after the sun set. It was great fun scaring the crap out of the guy who was \"it\" when he came near you but couldn't see you in the dark. If you beat him back to the telephone pole which was Gools, you had to say something like, \"My place, one, two, three\" to be safe.</p>\n          <p>After it rained and the street was dry, there'd remain a few large puddles. We'd ride our bikes over and over again through the puddles in a circle, until the circle was completely wet with no dry spots. Just for the fun of it.</p>\n          <p>In winter, we had our sleds out on the next street, Powderly Ave., where there was a short hill that we'd sled on down to the street. If the street was icy, our sleds would continue going and going for hundreds of feet. One kid lost part of his finger when he got it caught beneath a sled. I can't imagine how Bruce Parker felt that day.</p>\n          <p>We'd play tackle football on a neighbor's side yard. The field was only about 75 feet in length, but it did the trick. We loved whiffleball and my backyard was the place to play. You'd hit it into the oak trees over the second base/shortstop area and the team in the field would get an out if they caught the ball out of the tree before it touched the ground. The bad thing about my backyard was that we had a leaking cesspool that smelled really awful as the leaching field surrounding the cement container of the excrement couldn't keep it from wetting the lawn. The grass around the cesspool grew higher than the rest of the yard. On singles, we'd run past first base sloshing through the smelly cesspool water. I don't think we realized how gross it all was.</p>\n          <p>When it rained, we'd all get together at Alan Castaline's basement next door to play with his electric train set. Or, we'd play Monopoly, Risk and other games in my finished basement. We also collected baseball cards and \"flipped\" them either for distance or against a wall. The kid whose card was closest to the wall won all the rest of the cards. A \"leaner\" was the best flip. That was when a card leaned up against the wall and stayed leaning.</p>\n          <p>We played tag (touch) football in front of my house between the two telephone poles which were 100 feet from one another. The sidewalks were the out of bounds. We had a lot of fun playing tag football.</p>\n          <p>At night, in the spring or summer, under the street lights, we'd play \"cigarettes,\" a game. The person who was \"it\" threw a rubber ball high as he could into the air. Each kid was the name of a brand of cigarettes. So, if I was Winston, and the kid who threw the ball in the air called out \"Winston,\" I'd have to try to catch the ball before it hit the ground. If I was successful, I'd immediately call out another brand of cigarettes, e.g. Chesterfield, and whoever that brand represented had to catch the ball before it touched the ground. If the ball hit the ground, the kid had to get it and tell everyone to stop in their tracks. Then, the kid would be able to take \"three giant steps\" toward another kid, usually the closest kid to him, and try to throw the ball and hit the kid who couldn't step out of the way of the throw. If the kid got hit, he was the next one to be \"it\" and to throw the ball into the air while screaming the brand of a cigarette.</p>\n          <p>We also had new homes being built on the street next door. We'd go into the houses when the workmen weren't there, and have crab apple fights in the attic area which had 2 by 4's to stand on. Two teams of kids at each end of the attic area flinging crab apples at one another. We'd also steal wood and tools, which pissed off the workmen when they returned the next day. We'd use our stolen wood and tools to build tree huts in the woods or clubhouses in the backyards. One of our treehouses had wallpaper, real tar paper and roof shingles and was a two-story house.</p>\n          <p>As you can tell, we had a super time as children!</p>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n\n      <details class=\"full\">\n        <summary><span class=\"q\">Did you ever move as a child? What was that experience like?</span> <span class=\"more\">Read \u2192</span></summary>\n        <div class=\"body\">\n          <p>I was born in Roxbury, Massachusetts, then a poorer Jewish part of Boston, but in the 60's an African American part of town, as it is to this day. My parents and I lived in a small apartment on the third floor, if I remember correctly. I recall a car accident on the street just outside our apartment. I believe a person was struck, but I didn't get to see. In the summer it was very hot and stuffy. Rooms were small.</p>\n          <p>I recall when my mom was in labor with Michele, my dad's older sister, Aunt Lee, came to stay and watch me. What I remember explicitly is that she introduced me to soft-boiled eggs, which still gross me out! My maternal grandfather was in charge of the Roxbury post office, which I don't recall visiting. I do recall attending kindergarten at the Hecht House, a Jewish founded school not far away. In particular, I recall going there to a carnival and cutting my leg on a ride. Scary time. I also recall being a bit wild. I left the classroom on one occasion and climbed out a window of the classroom onto a balcony. I got into trouble for that misbehavior.</p>\n          <p>My family and my father's younger sister's family, Uncle Joe and Rhoda Oilshever, moved with us to a small house in a newly developed part of Randolph, a lower middle class suburb south of Boston. What particularly made me happy were the open spaces with a mile square of woods just behind my house, which became a favorite neighborhood playground for exploration. My friends and I made up \"The Game in the Woods,\" which was a combination of tag and hide and go seek. Loved that game.</p>\n          <p>We built a baseball field in a clearing down the street near the reservoir which fed several local towns. It is a wonderful memory, though a rocky and dangerous baseball field. For example, behind second base were larger boulders left in the Ice Age. I tripped over one and broke my arm at 10 years of age. I remember going to the town doctor. The waiting room was filled when I arrived, but I was in such pain that everyone let me go in first. The doctor sent me to Carney Hospital, a Catholic hospital a few towns over, where I had surgery. I recall wearing a cast on my right arm for weeks and weeks.</p>\n          <p>We played a lot of stickball on the street. Also, we played a lot of touch football in front of my house with the end zones being one telephone pole to the other, about 100 feet. The sidewalks were out of bounds. We played a lot of hide and seek. The best was at night when you couldn't see a darn thing unless illuminated by the streetlights. A blast.</p>\n          <p>I loved my neighborhood and the boys I made friends with in the neighborhood, all of whom I talk with every Monday at 10am PT and 1pm ET.</p>\n        </div>\n      </details>";
